{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 996 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b2c31cc56e461b322d808b692c0856739aada9eafbdc76a3c1686bc6db0c9588" :edb/db-before-t 995 :edb/db-after-t 996 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534308 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:51:12.535Z" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186110564 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418317 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186110564 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418318 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186110565 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418313 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186110566 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418315 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186110566 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418316 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186110567 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418314 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186110839 1050 1500.0 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186111423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186110263 13194139534308 17592186045417 false] [17592186111423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418307 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186111423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186111423 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{3}^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-0.7\\mu <X<2.7\\mu )=0.3256,$ find $\\mu $ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ | $\\,0.6664\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ | $\\,0.7019\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-0.7\\mu <X<2.7\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$*" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186111423 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(μ, 3^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-0.7μ < X < 2.7μ) = 0.3256$, find $μ$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ | $0.03$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.3$ | $0.6217$ | $0.6255$ | $0.6293$ |\n| $0.4$ | $0.6591$ | $0.6628$ | $0.6664$ |\n| $0.5$ | $0.6950$ | $0.6985$ | $0.7019$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-0.7μ < X < 2.7μ$ is symmetric about the mean $μ$.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534308 17592186045417 false] [17592186111423 1074 "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -1.7\\mu <X<\\mu +1.7\\mu )=0.3256,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-0.7\\mu <X<2.7\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -1.7\\mu <X<\\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.3256 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.3256 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.1628 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +1.7\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.1628 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +1.7\\mu )-0.5 & =0.1628 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.6628 \\\\ P(X\\le \\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.6628 \\\\ P(X\\le 2.7\\mu ) & =0.6628\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.7\\mu ) & =0.6628 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.7\\mu -\\mu }{3}) & =0.6628 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.7\\mu }{3}) & =0.6628 \\\\ \\frac{1.7\\mu }{3} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6628)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.42)=0.6628\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6628)=0.42.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.7\\mu }{3} & =0.42 \\\\ 1.7\\mu  & =1.26 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈0.741\\,18 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈0.74.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186111917 1064 "It's often helpful to use the symmetry properties of the normal distribution to help us determine unknown parameters.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following normal random variable with unknown variance $\\sigma^2{:}$\n\n$$\nX \\sim N(1, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nSuppose we also know that\n\n$$\nP(-2 \\lt X \\lt  4) = 0.8714.\n$$\n\nThe interval $-2 \\lt X \\lt  4$ and the area associated with the probability that $X$ lies within this interval are shown below.\n\n![](images/11/1101c2617de9f63394c7aa7da89fd7590ab401d792ba38e0c9c5fa8f8c4e9330.png)\n\nThe endpoints of the interval $(-2,4)$ both lie at a distance of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ from the mean $\\mu.$ So, this interval is symmetric about the mean, and we can write the given probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(\\mu - {\\color{red}{3}} \\lt X \\lt  \\mu + {\\color{red}{3}}) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the total shaded equals twice the area between the mean $\\mu$ and the right endpoint:\n\n$$\n2P(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\nP(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.4357.\n$$\n\nLet's write this probability in terms of $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{\\mu -\\mu }{\\sigma }<\\frac{X-\\mu }{\\sigma }<\\frac{\\mu +3-\\mu }{\\sigma })=0.4357\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<Z<\\frac{3}{\\sigma })=0.4357.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting this in terms of $\\Phi,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right)  - \\Phi(0) = 0.4357\n$$\n\nand since we know that $\\Phi(0) = 0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right)  - 0.5= 0.4357\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right) = 0.9357\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} = \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357).\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the given interval is symmetric about the mean:\n\n\\[\nP(-4 <        X <       2) = P(-1 - 3 <   X <   -1 + 3) \n\\]\n\nSo, the axis of symmetry for our distribution is [math]x=-1,[/math] as shown below. \n\nUsing its symmetry, we get\n\n\\[\nP(-4 <   X <   2) = 2 P(-1 <  X <   2),\n\\]\n\nand find the cumulative probability as follows:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\tP(-4 \\lt X \\lt  2)\t\t&= 0.8714\\\\[5pt]\n\t2 P(-1 \\lt X \\lt 2) \t\t&= 0.8714\\\\[5pt]\n\tP(-1 \\lt X \\lt 2) \t\t&= 0.4357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP(X \\lt 2) - P(X \\lt -1) \t&= 0.4357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP(X \\lt 2) - 0.5 \t\t&= 0.4357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP(X \\lt 2) \t\t\t&= 0.9357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP(X \\leq 2) \t\t\t&= 0.9357 \n\\end{align*}\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by [math]z[/math]-scoring:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\tP(X \\leq 2)\t\t\t\t\t\t&= 0.9357 \\\\[5pt]\n\tP \\left( Z \\leq \\dfrac{2 - (-1)}{\\sigma} \\right)\t&= 0.9357 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\Phi \\left( \\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} \\right)\t\t&= 0.9357 \\\\[5pt]\n\t\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} \t\t\t\t\t&= \\Phi^{-1} (0.9357 ) \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{align*}\n\n-\n\nLet's now take a look at the table of values for $\\Phi(z),$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9192 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9207 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9222 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9332 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9345 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9357 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9452 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9463 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9474 \\,$ |\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.52) = 0.9357 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357) = 1.52.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $\\sigma{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =1.52 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3}{1.52} \\\\ \\sigma  & \\approx 1.974\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534308 17592186045417 false] [17592186111917 1064 "It's often helpful to use the symmetry properties of the normal distribution to help us determine unknown parameters.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following normal random variable with unknown variance $\\sigma^2{:}$\n\n$$\nX \\sim N(1, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nSuppose we also know that\n\n$$\nP(-2 \\lt X \\lt  4) = 0.8714.\n$$\n\nThe interval $-2 \\lt X \\lt  4$ and the area associated with the probability that $X$ lies within this interval are shown below.\n\n![](images/11/1101c2617de9f63394c7aa7da89fd7590ab401d792ba38e0c9c5fa8f8c4e9330.png)\n\nThe endpoints of the interval $(-2,4)$ both lie at a distance of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ from the mean $\\mu.$ So, this interval is symmetric about the mean, and we can write the given probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(\\mu - {\\color{red}{3}} \\lt X \\lt  \\mu + {\\color{red}{3}}) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the total shaded equals twice the area between the mean $\\mu$ and the right endpoint:\n\n$$\n2P(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\nP(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.4357.\n$$\n\nLet's write this probability in terms of $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{\\mu -\\mu }{\\sigma }<\\frac{X-\\mu }{\\sigma }<\\frac{\\mu +3-\\mu }{\\sigma })=0.4357\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<Z<\\frac{3}{\\sigma })=0.4357.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting this in terms of $\\Phi,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right)  - \\Phi(0) = 0.4357\n$$\n\nand since we know that $\\Phi(0) = 0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right)  - 0.5= 0.4357\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right) = 0.9357\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} = \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357).\n$$\n\nLet's now take a look at the table of values for $\\Phi(z),$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9192 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9207 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9222 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9332 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9345 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9357 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9452 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9463 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9474 \\,$ |\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.52) = 0.9357 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357) = 1.52.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $\\sigma{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =1.52 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3}{1.52} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈1.974\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418283 1080 "1.24" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418283 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418283 1083 #uuid "0900ee31-7fc8-5a71-bfb0-567ce40867