Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(2,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X<-1)=0.3783,$ find $\\sigma ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3446\\,$ | $\\,0.3409\\,$ | $\\,0.3372\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3821\\,$ | $\\,0.3783\\,$ | $\\,0.3745\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4207\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ |" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418313 1072 #uuid "12a69b61-bda4-5cdc-b9c6-93090eb9484f" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418313 1074 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-1)=0.3783\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le -1)=0.3783\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -1) & =0.3783 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{-1-2}{\\sigma }) & =0.3783 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{3}{\\sigma }) & =0.3783 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.3783)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.31)=0.3783\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.3783)=-0.31.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =-0.31 \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈9.68\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places." 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418311 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418314 1069 "q-94890" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418314 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-0.8\\mu <X<2.8\\mu )=0.7458,$ find $\\mu ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-0.8\\mu <X<2.8\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$*" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418314 1072 #uuid "237d3289-35f7-5376-a92f-b552192446e9" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418314 1074 "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -1.8\\mu <X<\\mu +1.8\\mu )=0.7458,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-0.8\\mu <X<2.8\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -1.8\\mu <X<\\mu +1.8\\mu ) & =0.7458 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +1.8\\mu ) & =0.7458 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<2.8\\mu ) & =0.3729 \\\\ P(X<2.8\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.3729 \\\\ P(X<2.8\\mu )-0.5 & =0.3729 \\\\ P(X<2.8\\mu ) & =0.8729 \\\\ P(X\\le 2.8\\mu ) & =0.8729\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.8\\mu ) & =0.8729 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.8\\mu -\\mu }{5}) & =0.8729 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.8\\mu }{5}) & =0.8729 \\\\ \\frac{1.8\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8729)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.14)=0.8729\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8729)=1.14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.8\\mu }{5} & =1.14 \\\\ 1.8\\mu  & =5.7 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈3.166\\,67 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈3.17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418306 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418315 1069 "q-342034" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418315 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(|X|<3)=0.5468,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ |\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418315 1072 #uuid "4375ac91-f4dd-57cf-abab-b28e66baa865" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418315 1074 "First, we express the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(|X|<3)=P(-3<X<3).\n$$\n\nThen, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3<X<3) & =0.5468 \\\\ 2P(0<X<3) & =0.5468 \\\\ P(0<X<3) & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<3)-P(X<0) & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<3)-0.5 & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<3) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(X\\le 3) & =0.7734\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.7734 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{3}{\\sigma }) & =0.7734 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)=0.7734\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)=0.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =0.75 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3}{0.75} \\\\ \\sigma  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(4)^{2}=16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418308 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418316 1069 "q-341988" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418316 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-1.5<X<1.5)=0.3472,$ use the table to find $\\sigma ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418316 1072 #uuid "7317b079-775e-5316-a7ce-180fb3a0e8c0" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418316 1074 "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.5<X<1.5) & =0.3472 \\\\ 2P(0<X<1.5) & =0.3472 \\\\ P(0<X<1.5) & =0.1736 \\\\ P(X<1.5)-P(X<0) & =0.1736 \\\\ P(X<1.5)-0.5 & =0.1736 \\\\ P(X<1.5) & =0.6736 \\\\ P(X\\le 1.5) & =0.6736\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1.5) & =0.6736 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{1.5-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.6736 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.6736 \\\\ \\frac{1.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6736)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.45)=0.6736\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6736)=0.45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.5}{\\sigma } & =0.45 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1.5}{0.45} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈3.33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418310 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418317 1069 "q-94826" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418317 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{4}^{2})$ with $P(X<2.5)=0.7881,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418317 1072 #uuid "9f740b39-58c8-58b2-9755-cce906b6c35a" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418317 1074 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2.5)=0.7881\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le 2.5)=0.7881\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.5) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.5-\\mu }{4}) & =0.7881 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2.5-\\mu }{4}) & =0.7881 \\\\ \\frac{2.5-\\mu }{4} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.80)=0.7881\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)=0.80.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.5-\\mu }{4} & =0.80 \\\\ 2.5-\\mu  & =3.2 \\\\ \\mu  & =-0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418309 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418318 1069 "q-340851" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418318 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{4}^{2})$ with $P(X\\ge 3)=0.3446,$ find $\\mu .$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418318 1072 #uuid "b07a943e-feb9-5e5c-823f-0048a8326fe6" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true] [17592186418318 1074 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\ge 3) \\\\  & =1-0.3446 \\\\  & =0.6554\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =0.6554 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{3-\\mu }{4}) & =0.6554 \\\\ Φ\\,(\\frac{3-\\mu }{4}) & =0.6554 \\\\ \\frac{3-\\mu }{4} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6554)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have \n\n$$\nΦ(0.40)=0.6554\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6554)=0.40.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3-\\mu }{4} & =0.40 \\\\ 3-\\mu  & =1.60 \\\\ \\mu  & =1.40.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534308 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0900ee31-7fc8-5a71-bfb0-567ce40867a6" 17592186418283 "answer-1c4717ce-46ec-5dfd-a87b-f3dbc1bd41c7" 17592186418284 "answer-29c5beaa-2bd5-5a17-8ed7-0f68efb5d37f" 17592186418285 "answer-2c678ed7-d187-5b4d-b65e-df460c846e37" 17592186418286 "answer-37635532-f28a-53e6-ae9d-3b79e5b093b8" 17592186418287 "answer-3e523356-51e6-5eb9-8b16-754f02547625" 17592186418288 "answer-4e35a48b-83ad-5a61-a3f1-7f195f1064b7" 17592186418289 "answer-53d6307a-364e-5b45-a243-85e1a8f8cc2d" 17592186418290 "answer-53fe27ad-e859-55d8-9328-3819e6132d39" 17592186418291 "answer-5894f257-4ebc-5d56-a70d-cf0e5f88980b" 17592186418292 "answer-5cefab71-1972-502d-b0e9-4eeba025fe95" 17592186418293 "answer-5d6f0a73-e2e0-5875-93f7-c3a9c8822865" 17592186418294 "answer-6d43d3be-0f5b-5f12-af73-e68463fd21f6" 17592186418295 "answer-929c9f87-8068-54c6-a062-0aad2ba05db7" 17592186418296 "answer-9cdaf6c6-dec3-5b38-a91c-2be375299096" 17592186418297 "answer-c77996be-b841-5a9e-9afd-b3001a00c48c" 17592186418298 "answer-cf7c4b24-62e9-5b3c-92b0-6a9e6302154f" 17592186418299 "answer-d88c6cd0-f17d-5079-9478-4d437c156ca5" 17592186418300 "answer-dd8135e4-0878-5169-b0ef-52cf2b36e46a" 17592186418301 "answer-e35e3297-c505-59cf-87e9-2b40589a0852" 17592186418302 "answer-e6d35c5e-62bc-5eec-b8a3-467f34a66e46" 17592186418303 "answer-f6c68472-5317-5646-8c48-47295237b963" 17592186418304 "answer-fa154a6e-7b02-